    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Secularism seeks to conduct human affairs on naturalistic considerations and is most commonly understood as the separation of religion from civil affairs and the state; it is concerned with minimising the role of religion rather than disproving it, and may be hostile, neutral or hospitable to religion.</li><li>The French, Turkish, American and Indian models differ greatly: the American emphasis falls on avoiding an established religion and on freedom of belief, the French model is interventionist, and the Indian model combines equality of citizens before the law with some separation.</li><li>The word was coined in 1851 by George Holyoake, who found “atheism” too aggravating and wanted a term that allowed cooperation with believers, but its origins have been traced to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> itself and through Christian history.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>George Holyoake, who used the word “secularism” in its modern sense in 1851, did so because he found “atheism” too aggravating; he wanted a stance that did not necessarily reject religion and so allowed cooperation with believers.</p></aside>
    </section>

